  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1253 PM EST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nNow, with the area under a broad trough, various shortwaves dive \nsouthward through the Great Lakes region continuing to keep the lake \neffect machine invigorated into Sunday. During this time, the cold \nair at 850 mb lags behind and slowly drops southward finally \nreaching the lower Great Lakes Thursday night or early Friday \nmorning. As a shortwave traverses Lk MI midday today, this pushes \nthe lake effect rain area westward, into LOTs area. Some wavering of \ntrajectories wiggles the main band eastward for a short period of \ntime early Friday morning and possibly again around 00z Friday \nevening providing additional uncertainty to the forecast. Periods of \ndrier air under the convective moisture column as well as DGZ \nmoistening issues at times during this period likely impinge on \nrates and ratios even with some negative theta-e lapse rates and \nlake-induced instability to work with. The saturation issues\ncould cause some drizzle to occur at times. If the band sits in\nany one spot in our area, it could snow, but then the rates are\nin question given questions about column saturation issues. The\nfinal vigorous vort max pushes through the area Saturday \nmorning and this is also the time that 850 mb temperatures warm \na degree or two and 1000-700 mb shear looks to increase by the \ntime we get to Saturday and so even though trajectories attempt \nto gain a more northwest direction, many factors contribute to \nweaker snow, especially as trajectories waver minimizing the \nband's residence time in any one location. Initially, it was \nlooking like Friday's snow was going to be the more impactful \ndose. However, if there is saturation issues through the column,\nthen it may be Saturday produces more accumulating snow, but \nSaturday's snowfall total ceiling is fairly low given the above \npoints. Right now, only have a couple tenths of snow for either \ntime period. Additionally, MaxTs are expected to reach back into\nthe 40s both Friday and Saturday and those should limit \naccumulations especially farther from the lake. Roadways are \nstarting warm from our recent above normal temp spell and it \nwould take heavy rates to get them to accreting temperature. \nGiven the above points, if it snows, it would probably be to \ngrassy areas only. \n\nAs surface high pressure and mid level continue to build into the \narea from the west, trajectories gain more of a southerly component \nshutting off the lake effect machine and drying out the area. Given \nthe place of the surface high overtime, a transition to a warm \nadvection pattern takes over and high temperatures are able to \ngradually warm back through the 40s to the 50s by Tuesday or \nWednesday. However, low temperatures overnight will be at or below \nfreezing starting even tonight and won't rise above 32 again until \nlate week next week.",
